  </div>

  <h2 id="boundaries">Boundaries that are constantly confused</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-amber">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>These two</th><th>Differ because…</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Azure RBAC vs Microsoft Entra role</td><td>Resources vs the tenant. Different blades, different administrators.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Azure Policy vs RBAC vs resource lock</td><td>What it may look like vs who may touch it vs whether it can be deleted.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Exemption vs exclusion</td><td>Exemption suppresses the result with a justification; exclusion removes the scope from the assignment.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Service endpoint vs private endpoint</td><td>Subnet identity presented to a public endpoint vs a private IP in your subnet. Only the private endpoint is reachable from on-premises.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Security admin rule vs NSG rule</td><td>Central governance at the virtual network, evaluated first, with an Always-allow action NSGs do not have.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Access restrictions vs virtual network integration</td><td>Inbound vs outbound, on App Service and Functions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>TDE vs Always Encrypted vs dynamic data masking</td><td>Stolen media vs privileged insiders vs presentation only.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>User delegation SAS vs service SAS vs account SAS</td><td>Signed by Entra vs by the account key, and therefore revocable in three very different ways.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Defender for Servers P1 vs P2</td><td>P2 adds agentless scanning, JIT, FIM, compliance, OS baselines and premium vulnerability management.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Defender CSPM vs Defender EASM</td><td>Looking at resources you own vs assets that resolve to you on the internet.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Analytics tier vs data lake tier</td><td>Full detection features and included queries vs cheap long retention and restricted features.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Automation rule vs playbook</td><td>Orchestration inside Sentinel vs a Logic App that reaches outside it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sign-in frequency vs continuous access evaluation</td><td>A timer vs event-driven near-real-time revocation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Restricted Content Discovery vs Restricted Access Control</td><td>Discoverability, permissions untouched vs a genuine access boundary.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Foundry guardrail vs Defender real-time protection</td><td>Screening text vs stopping a tool invocation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Immutable vault vs multi-user authorization vs soft delete</td><td>Cannot be shortened vs needs a second approver vs recoverable after deletion.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="traps">Traps that cost marks</h2>

  <h3>Identity and governance</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Owner does not grant data access.</strong> Storage and Key Vault under RBAC need a separate data-plane role.</li>
    <li><strong>There is no deny you can assign</strong> in Azure RBAC. To stop an action, use Azure Policy.</li>
    <li><strong>A <code>ReadOnly</code> lock blocks listing storage account keys</strong>, because that call is a write.</li>
    <li><strong>A lock never protects data</strong> — it is a control-plane control only.</li>
    <li><strong>Locks cannot be applied at a management group.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Security defaults and Conditional Access are mutually exclusive.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Number matching in Microsoft Authenticator cannot be turned off.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Authenticator push is not phishing-resistant.</strong> Passkeys, Windows Hello for Business and certificate-based authentication are.</li>
    <li><strong>Application permissions always require admin consent</strong>; delegated ones may not.</li>
    <li><strong>DeployIfNotExists needs a managed identity and a remediation task</strong> — creating the assignment fixes nothing that already exists.</li>
    <li><strong>Purge protection cannot be turned off</strong>, and <strong>infrastructure encryption can only be chosen at creation</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Break-glass accounts must be excluded</strong> from Conditional Access and left out of PIM.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Storage, data and network</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A private endpoint without a private DNS zone does nothing</strong> — clients still resolve the public address.</li>
    <li><strong>A private endpoint does not disable the public path.</strong> Set public network access to Disabled as well.</li>
    <li><strong>Storage firewall rules reject private IP ranges.</strong> Use a service endpoint or private endpoint for internal subnets.</li>
    <li><strong>"Allow Azure services and resources to access this server" means any Azure tenant</strong>, not just yours.</li>
    <li><strong>Private endpoints are per sub-resource</strong> — one for blob does nothing for file.</li>
    <li><strong>Inbound is subnet NSG then NIC NSG; outbound is NIC then subnet.</strong> Both must allow.</li>
    <li><strong>Security admin rules exempt several subnets</strong> — Application Gateway, Bastion, Azure Firewall, Route Server, VPN Gateway, Virtual WAN and ExpressRoute gateway.</li>
    <li><strong>Only one security admin configuration per region per network manager.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>IDPS, TLS inspection and URL filtering are Azure Firewall Premium only</strong>, and are configured in a firewall policy, not on the firewall.</li>
    <li><strong>Database-level SQL firewall rules are evaluated before server-level ones</strong>, and can only be created with T-SQL.</li>
    <li><strong>NSG flow logs are retired on 30 September 2027</strong>; use virtual network flow logs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Compute and applications</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Integrity monitoring requires vTPM, which requires trusted launch.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Bastion SKUs upgrade but never downgrade.</strong> Session recording and private-only deployment are Premium only.</li>
    <li><strong>JIT is Defender for Servers Plan 2</strong>, and it edits NSG and firewall rules rather than removing public IPs.</li>
    <li><strong>Agentless malware scanning is Plan 2 only</strong>, even though other agentless capabilities come with Defender CSPM.</li>
    <li><strong>File integrity monitoring is not on by default</strong> even in Plan 2.</li>
    <li><strong>AKS local accounts bypass Microsoft Entra entirely.</strong> Disable them.</li>
    <li><strong>The ACR admin user is a shared password.</strong> Disable it and use <code>AcrPull</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Function keys are not authentication</strong>, and the master key is administrative.</li>
    <li><strong>Access restrictions must also be applied to the <code>scm</code> site</strong>, or the deployment endpoint stays open.</li>
    <li><strong>WAF: tune with exclusions, not by disabling rules</strong>, and start in Detection mode.</li>
    <li><strong>An APIM subscription key identifies a subscription, not a user.</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Posture, monitoring and AI</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The compliance dashboard needs a paid plan</strong>; MCSB recommendations alone come free.</li>
    <li><strong>Filter with a data collection rule at the agent</strong> — filtering in a query after ingestion has already cost you.</li>
    <li><strong>CEF lands in <code>CommonSecurityLog</code></strong>, with non-standard fields in <code>AdditionalExtensions</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Playbooks need their own managed identity and role assignments</strong>, and Sentinel needs Automation Contributor on their resource group.</li>
    <li><strong>Security Copilot never exceeds the signed-in user's permissions.</strong> Fix the service role, not the Copilot role.</li>
    <li><strong>An agent's guardrail overrides the model's</strong>, and Microsoft Default guardrails cannot be edited.</li>
    <li><strong>Conditional Access for agents targets users for on-behalf-of flows</strong> and agent identities for app-only flows.</li>
    <li><strong>429 is the rate limit; 403 is the quota.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Copilot Studio real-time protection covers generative orchestration agents only</strong> — classic agents are skipped.</li>
    <li><strong>The Data and AI security dashboard stays empty</strong> without Defender CSPM with sensitive data discovery, Defender for Storage, Defender for Databases and AI threat protection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="scenario-lookup">Scenario-to-answer lookup</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="tbl t-slate">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>The scenario says…</th><th>The answer is…</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>"Administrators must request access and be approved before using the role"</td><td>PIM eligible assignment with approval required</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Validate the impact before enforcing"</td><td>Conditional Access report-only mode</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Why did this policy apply to this one user?"</td><td>Conditional Access What If</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Prevent anyone creating a resource that breaks this rule"</td><td>Azure Policy with the <code>Deny</code> effect</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Fix the hundreds of resources that already break it"</td><td><code>DeployIfNotExists</code> or <code>Modify</code>, plus a remediation task</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Accept this non-compliance temporarily, with a recorded reason"</td><td>A policy exemption, category Waiver, with an expiry</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Remove the connection string from the application"</td><td>A managed identity plus the appropriate data role</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"The deployment pipeline holds a client secret that keeps expiring"</td><td>Workload identity federation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Grant access to one secret only"</td><td>Azure RBAC at the secret's scope, not an access policy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"A SAS leaked; invalidate it without disrupting other apps"</td><td>Delete the stored access policy, or revoke the user delegation key</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Stop anyone using storage account keys"</td><td><code>allowSharedKeyAccess = false</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>"The DBA must administer but must never read the salary column"</td><td>Always Encrypted</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Only calls from this subnet may reach the storage account"</td><td>Service endpoint with a virtual network rule, or a private endpoint</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Block inbound SSH everywhere, with exceptions for two networks"</td><td>Virtual Network Manager security admin rules at two priorities</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Inspect outbound TLS traffic for malware signatures"</td><td>Azure Firewall Premium with TLS inspection and IDPS</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Contractors need one internal app, not network access"</td><td>Microsoft Entra Private Access, per-app access</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Keep an audit trail of what administrators did in the remote session"</td><td>Azure Bastion Premium session recording</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Enforce the password policy inside 400 servers, including on-premises"</td><td>Azure Arc plus Azure Machine Configuration</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Stop unsigned containers being deployed"</td><td>The Azure Policy add-on for Kubernetes, deny effect, approved registries</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"A rogue administrator must not be able to delete backups alone"</td><td>Multi-user authorization with a Resource Guard</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Discover internet-facing assets we do not know we own"</td><td>Defender External Attack Surface Management</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Reduce Sentinel ingestion cost without losing the events we alert on"</td><td>A data collection rule with a transformation, and move noisy tables to the data lake tier with summary rules</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Analysts triage incidents and run approved playbooks, nothing more"</td><td>Microsoft Sentinel Responder plus Playbook Operator</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Search audit data from two years ago"</td><td>A search job over long-term retention</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Copilot surfaces a site that is still under permissions review"</td><td>Restricted Content Discovery</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Stop a subverted agent from running a destructive tool"</td><td>A custom real-time protection rule in Defender Security for AI</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"One project must not consume the whole model quota"</td><td>AI Gateway token limits in API Management</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="study-plan">A final-two-weeks plan</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>Days 1–2.</strong> Re-read <a href={href('security-foundations')}>Foundations</a> and <a href={href('skill-1-identity-access-governance')}>Skill 1</a>. Build a PIM eligible assignment and a Conditional Access policy in report-only mode in a trial tenant.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 3–5.</strong> <a href={href('skill-2-storage-databases-networking')}>Skill 2</a>, the widest domain. Create a storage account with a private endpoint and no public access, and prove to yourself that DNS is what makes it work.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 6–8.</strong> <a href={href('skill-3-secure-compute')}>Skill 3</a>. Deploy a VM with trusted launch behind Bastion, turn on JIT, and read the Defender for Servers plan comparison until P1 and P2 are automatic.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 9–10.</strong> <a href={href('skill-4-security-posture-monitoring')}>Skill 4</a>. Stand up a Sentinel workspace, install one content hub solution, connect Azure Activity, and write a data collection rule that filters.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 11–12.</strong> The <a href={href('ai-agent-security')}>AI &amp; agent security deep dive</a>, twice. Then re-read the Microsoft Learn page for each preview feature, because this is where the product has moved most recently.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 13.</strong> This page. The numbers table, the boundaries table and the traps, out loud.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 14.</strong> Re-read the official <a href={SITE.studyGuide} target="_blank" rel="noopener">skills measured</a> bullet by bullet and say one sentence about each. Anything you cannot, look up tonight. Then stop.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2 id="on-the-day">On the day</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Case studies first or last, but decide beforehand.</strong> They carry several questions on one scenario; reading the exhibit twice costs less than switching context twice.</li>
    <li><strong>Mark and move.</strong> 120 minutes is comfortable if you do not stall. Flag anything that takes more than ninety seconds and come back.</li>
    <li><strong>Answer every question.</strong> There is no negative marking.</li>
    <li><strong>On yes/no statement sets, evaluate each statement independently.</strong> They are deliberately written so that two look identical and one word differs.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch for "least privilege", "minimal administrative effort" and "without affecting users".</strong> Those three phrases pick the answer more often than any technical detail.</li>
    <li><strong>Trust the current name.</strong> If an option uses a name you only know from an old blog post, it is more likely to be the distractor.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="tip" title="And the meta-point">
    <p>
      SC-500 is an engineering exam about <em>reducing standing access and closing default-open paths</em>. When you
      genuinely cannot decide, pick the option that gives somebody less, for less time, over a narrower path. It is
      right far more often than chance.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout type="warn" title="Verify before you rely on any of this">
    <p>
      These notes target the skills measured as of <strong>{SITE.skillsMeasured}</strong>. Microsoft revises both the
      products and the objectives without notice, and the AI security surface in particular has already moved since this
      exam launched. Re-read the official <a href={SITE.studyGuide} target="_blank" rel="noopener">SC-500 study
      guide</a> before you book, and check the Microsoft Learn page for anything here that decides your answer.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</NoteLayout>
